# Project Context: Uncertainty-Aware Sequential Detection of Imminent Temperature Excursions

## Technical Stack & Standards
- Language: Python 3.11+
- Libraries: numpy, scipy, pandas, matplotlib, pytest, torch (for baselines)
- Code Style: Strict type hints, comprehensive docstrings (Google style), modular architecture.
- Testing: Every module must have corresponding `pytest` tests in `tests/`. Fixed random seeds for reproducibility.
- Configuration: Use dataclasses or YAML configuration files.

## Mathematical Conventions
- State transition: $x(t+1) = F x(t) + w(t)$, $w \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q)$ (local-linear-trend or OU).
- Observations: $y(t) = x(t) + e(t)$ when $m(t)=1$, where $m(t)=1$ denotes "observed".
- Missingness: $P(m(t)=1 \mid x(t)) = \text{logistic}(g_0 + g_1(x(t) - \mu))$.
- Risk: $r(t) = P\left(\max_{k=1\dots H} x(t+k) > \tau \;\Big\vert{}\; y(1\dots t), m(1\dots t)\right)$ with credible interval $[r_{\text{lo}}, r_{\text{hi}}]$.
- Decision Rules: Three-way decision framework (ALARM / VERIFY / WAIT) with gap-stratified conformal calibration and e-process false-alarm control.

## Execution Rules
- Always write or update tests alongside code implementation.
- Do not invent library functions; implement clean fallback logic if necessary.
- State underlying assumptions clearly in docstrings and comments.
